  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist. I didn't open the dashboard to check how the panels render, and I haven't seen the rule fire.\n\n**What the data holds:** `kibana_sample_data_logs` is web access-log data with `timestamp` as its time field. The fields I used are:\n- `response`, a string status code (use `response.keyword` for grouping).\n- `bytes`, the response size.\n- `extension.keyword`, the file type requested.\n- `geo.src`, the source country.\n- Others include `clientip`, `host`, `machine.os`, `referer`, `url` and `agent`.\n\n**Dashboard:** \"EFF-4d1de3d4 Web logs overview\" has 5 panels:\n- Total requests (metric)\n- Requests over time (area)\n- Responses by status code (pie)\n- Average bytes by extension (bar)\n- Top 10 source countries (table)\n\nIt is at http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-4d1de3d4-web-logs-overview-cd9e0f966cf9.\n\n**Alert rule:** \"EFF-4d1de3d4 5xx spike\" (id `c50d5d4c-fc5f-4953-a999-e932d453b963`) is an `.es-query` rule. It runs every minute and fires when more than 10 documents with a 5xx `response.keyword` arrive in a 5-minute window. It matches 5xx with the regexp `5[0-9][0-9]`. The tool creates rules disabled, so I enabled this one so it can fire.\n\nThe rule has no action connector attached, so when it fires it only shows up as an alert in Kibana and doesn't notify anyone.",
